1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,450
Penny Post. Every Seeker came with an address, a .skr name. Nobody writes to it. This is a letterbox for your wallet, and the post comes once a day.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:11,650
A letterbox for your wallet. One button, and it asks my wallet to connect.

3
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:20,221
Connected. Every signature in this app goes through Mobile Wallet Adapter. The wallet on screen is Solana Mobile's reference test wallet. Any wallet that speaks Mobile Wallet Adapter does the same job.

4
00:00:20,271 --> 00:00:33,221
One card for everyone. Somebody asked every Seeker a question and put up 300 SKR for whoever answers. I open the envelope and pick my answer.

5
00:00:33,271 --> 00:00:37,221
The wallet opens and I sign. Every transaction is dry-run first, so one the program would refuse never opens the wallet.

6
00:00:37,271 --> 00:00:50,721
The program checks my Seeker Genesis Token. Ten wallets on one phone still get one answer. When the day closes, every phone that answered gets an equal share. Mine is about 45 SKR, for ten seconds.

7
00:00:50,771 --> 00:00:58,721
Yesterday's share is waiting. There is a new question, and about 45 SKR from yesterday's. For this scene the local chain's clock and the phone's clock were moved forward a day.

8
00:00:58,771 --> 00:01:11,221
Collect and answer together. The same approval collects yesterday's share and sends today's answer.

9
00:01:11,271 --> 00:01:24,721
One tap a day, and it pays. A builder can put one question in front of every Seeker and pay only the phones that respond.

10
00:01:24,771 --> 00:01:35,721
A card from a stranger. They paid my price, 10 SKR, to reach me. The card is face down. I can see who sent it and what is inside, and nothing else.

11
00:01:35,771 --> 00:01:40,221
The postage waits in escrow. If I ignore the card, their SKR goes back to them after a week. A letterbox takes three paid cards a day, at a price its owner sets, so it cannot be flooded.

12
00:01:40,271 --> 00:01:54,721
9 SKR is mine. The card turns over. A tenth of the postage was burned. It was sealed to a key that never leaves this phone.

13
00:01:54,771 --> 00:02:04,721
To a .skr name or a wallet. The card at the top is the real thing, filled in as I go.

14
00:02:04,771 --> 00:02:19,721
What it costs, before I sign. I write the back with my finger. Postage is 12 SKR, which is this reader's price, and it arrives at eight tomorrow morning. Seeker owners also get one free stamp a day.

15
00:02:19,771 --> 00:02:29,721
In the box. Sealed, so only their phone can read it. If they never open it, my postage comes back.

16
00:02:29,771 --> 00:02:36,221
I set the price. A stranger pays what I ask, and I keep 90% when I open their card.

17
00:02:36,271 --> 00:02:46,221
Ask every Seeker. Reach Seeker owners without an airdrop. Put up SKR, ask one question, and it goes to the phones that answer.

18
00:02:46,271 --> 00:02:54,221
Live on Solana devnet, with a signed APK anyone can install. 31 unit tests and 54 end-to-end tests, run against real SKR and real Seeker Genesis Tokens on a fork of mainnet. Six faults found in our own review, fixed and written up. The post comes once a day.
